# gitleaks — triaged historical findings (NFR-034).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS. gitleaks scans all 482 commits, not the working tree, so a credential that was committed
# and later removed keeps failing the build forever. The nine fingerprints below are the dev-tier credentials
# that the 18.B1 R2 purge took OUT of the tree; the code is clean at HEAD and a fresh checkout contains none
# of them. Each line is one finding, triaged individually.
#
# WHY NOT REWRITE HISTORY. Purging these with filter-repo rewrites every SHA on a shared remote and breaks
# every open branch, checkout and PR link, to remove dev-only values from a PRIVATE repository. That trade is
# not worth it here. It WOULD be worth it for a production credential or anything reachable from outside a
# developer's machine — if that ever lands, rewrite the history, do not add a line to this file.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is not a way to make a finding go away. A fingerprint pins one secret in one
# commit at one line: a new leak, the same secret in a new commit, or the same commit at a different line all
# still fail. Adding an entry means "I looked at this and it is historical", so do that before adding one.
#
# STILL OUTSTANDING, and not fixable by any file in this repo: these are the dev Compose passwords, and
# infra/compose/.env still uses the same values today. History exposure plus a still-current value means the
# real remediation is rotating them in the dev environment — see docs/AUDIT-2026-08-09-PLATFORM-WIDE.md §5a.
# Severity is low (private repo, local-only services, no production reach), which is why this is recorded
# rather than treated as an incident.

